 "title": "Mastering Negotiation Skills",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator explains that the biggest mistake in negotiation is trying to force the other person to say yes rather than understanding their needs. The core lesson is to negotiate smarter by understanding concerns, adjusting deliverables instead of just dropping prices, and knowing your limits.",
 "key_points": [
  "Do not immediately defend your price if a client says your 10,000 rupees service is too expensive.",
  "Ask: 'Is your main concern the budget or the value that you are getting?'",
  "Never lower your price without reducing the scope (e.g. if they want to pay 6,000 instead of 10,000, offer fewer deliverables).",
  "Know when to walk away because being too desperate makes you more likely to accept bad terms.",
  "Know your limits before entering a negotiation.",
  "The goal is not to defeat the other person, but to create a deal that works for both sides."
 ],
 "content": "Negotiation is an art. And the biggest mistake people make while negotiating is they try to convince the other person to say yes. But here's what you should do instead. Ask before you negotiate. If a client says your 10,000 rupees service is too expensive, don't immediately defend your price. Ask, is your main concern the budget or the value that you're getting? The better you ask, the more you understand. Second, never lower your price without reducing the scope. If they want to pay 6,000 instead of 10,000, offer fewer deliverables. Don't give the same work for less money. Number third, and the most important, know when to walk away. The more desperate you are for a deal, the more likely you are to accept bad terms. Know your limits before entering a negotiation. And remember, the goal is not to defeat the other person. It's to create a deal that works for both the side. Negotiate smarter, not harder. For more such videos, you know what to do. Bye. Bye. Text shown on screen: Negotiation art biggest people make while negotiating try to convince other person say yes here's what you should do instead ask before you negotiate if a client your 10000 rupees service is expensive don't immediately defend price ask main concern budget value getting better you understand second never lower price without reducing scope if they want pay 6000 instead 10000 offer fewer deliverables don't give same work less money and most important know when walk away more desperate you for deal likely accept bad terms know limits before entering negotiation and remember goal not defeat person create deal that works both side negotiate smarter not harder videos you know what do bye bye",
